r1_priorart.md:432:## 6. Mandatory complexity and correctness audit
r1_priorart.md:436:**Representation.** Is the object genuinely stored in polynomial space, or merely described by a short program that later expands? Are all required operations efficient on that representation?
r1_priorart.md:438:**Coefficient access.** Does the method require sequential coefficients up to `p`, a dense truncation at `K`, or exponentially many terms after sectioning? Section 2.3 rules out individual coefficient evaluation as the missing advantage here.
r1_priorart.md:440:**Field assumptions.** Does a theorem require a known prime, an algebraically closed field, a characteristic bound, or invertible denominators? Prove the needed ring version or report factor-revealing nonunits.
r1_priorart.md:442:**Hidden inputs.** Is any step using `p`, `q`, `phi(N)`, `lambda(N)`, an unknown order, a hidden lattice basis, or informative samples supplied by an oracle? State exactly how it is obtained.
r1_priorart.md:444:**Numerical cost.** An error tolerance `2^(-poly(n))` uses polynomially many bits. An error tolerance `2^(-p)` needs order-`p` bits. A tiny error written with a short exponent is not automatically cheap to achieve. Conversely, do not call every exponentially small error an exponential-precision requirement.
r1_priorart.md:446:**State-space cost.** A short diffusion rule, tensor description, or matrix-power expression is not enough. Bound the represented state, ranks, contractions, and the cost of the final observable.
r1_priorart.md:448:**Degree and root bounds.** Check polynomial identity-test assumptions when formal degree is exponential. Over a finite ring, nonzero polynomials can vanish at every scalar point. Auxiliary prime moduli also change coefficient divisibility: reducing modulo an unrelated prime does not preserve vanishing modulo `N` for free.
r1_priorart.md:450:**Randomness.** Bound the success probability and cost per attempt, including constructing the distribution and rejecting bad outputs. Distinguish worst-case, average-case, and restricted-family claims.
r1_priorart.md:452:**Certificates.** A candidate factor is cheaply verifiable. That does not bound search time or certify an average-case/polynomial-time theorem. A decoder's correctness with ideal samples does not validate the source of those samples.
r1_priorart.md:454:**Novelty.** Compare with the closest specific method. A new notation, a different implementation of a known identity, or a known reduction rediscovered here is not an asymptotic breakthrough. It can still be useful scaffolding for the next modification.
r1_priorart.md:458:## 7. Workspace and required deliverables
r1_priorart.md:502:## 8. Primary-source reading list
r1_priorart.md:506:**[AKS]** Manindra Agrawal, Neeraj Kayal, Nitin Saxena. *PRIMES is in P*. Annals of Mathematics 160 (2004), 781–793. [Paper](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/wp-content/uploads/2016/02/primality_journal.pdf). Focus on Lemma 2.1 and the quotient-ring reduction in Section 2; distinguish primality detection from factor extraction.
r1_priorart.md:508:**[BGS]** Alin Bostan, Pierrick Gaudry, Éric Schost. *Linear Recurrences with Polynomial Coefficients and Application to Integer Factorization and Cartier–Manin Operator*. SIAM Journal on Computing 36(6) (2007), 1777–1806. [Author-hosted paper](https://mathexp.eu/bostan/publications/BoGaSc07.pdf). This directly connects distant recurrence terms, product computations, and classical factorization. Audit the dependence on the recurrence index.
r1_priorart.md:510:**[Harvey]** David Harvey. *An exponent one-fifth algorithm for deterministic integer factorisation*. arXiv:2010.05450 (2020). [Record](https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.05450). A rigorous `N^(1/5+o(1))` classical factoring construction; a useful structured-search baseline, not polynomial in input bit length.
r1_priorart.md:512:**[HH]** David Harvey, Markus Hittmeir. *A log-log speedup for exponent one-fifth deterministic integer factorisation*. arXiv:2105.11105 (2021). [Record](https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.11105). Study the arithmetic structure and the actual batching operation, not just its asymptotic headline.
r1_priorart.md:514:**[Sparse]** Joris van der Hoeven, Grégoire Lecerf. *Sparse polynomial interpolation: Faster strategies over finite fields*. Author-hosted final version dated 2 May 2024. [Text](https://www.texmacs.org/joris/ffsparse/ffsparse.html). Relevant to projections, support reconstruction, and circuit access. Its field, sparsity, characteristic, and root-of-unity assumptions require explicit checking for our setting.
r1_priorart.md:516:**[Tal]** Yann Tal. *Computing Modular Factorials Below the Square-Root Barrier*. ECCC TR26-211, September 2026 preprint. [Record and paper access](https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2026/211/). A recent lead involving known prime/prime-power moduli, Jacobi sums, and recursive factorial computations. Treat the claims as preprint results to inspect, and preserve the known-modulus/divisor conditions. No general unknown-factor-modulus speedup is asserted by this brief.
r1_priorart.md:518:**[Coppersmith]** Don Coppersmith. *Small Solutions to Polynomial Equations, and Low Exponent RSA Vulnerabilities*. Journal of Cryptology 10 (1997), 233–260. [Publisher record](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s001459900030). Use to specify exactly what partial factor information would suffice, rather than casually assuming that arbitrary correlated bits are usable.
r1_priorart.md:520:**[Regev]** Oded Regev. *An Efficient Quantum Factoring Algorithm*. arXiv:2308.06572 (2023; consult current version). [Record](https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.06572). A source for the hidden-relation formulation and the separation between quantum information acquisition and classical postprocessing.
r1_priorart.md:522:**[Pilatte]** Cédric Pilatte. *Unconditional correctness of recent quantum algorithms for factoring and computing discrete logarithms*. arXiv:2404.16450; Forum of Mathematics, Pi 14 (2026), e5. [Record](https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.16450). Structural existence/correctness results for suitable modified constructions; do not equate existence of short relations with constructive classical access to them.
r1_priorart.md:524:**[EG]** Martin Ekerå, Joel Gärtner. *Extending Regev's factoring algorithm to compute discrete logarithms*. arXiv:2311.05545 (2023; consult current version). [Record](https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.05545). Relevant to what approximate lattice information suffices for classical recovery. Extract the exact noise, distribution, and precision requirements.
r1_priorart.md:526:**[Gao]** Yiming Gao, Yansong Feng, Honggang Hu, Yanbin Pan. *On Factoring and Power Divisor Problems via Rank-3 Lattices and the Second Vector*. arXiv:2512.19076 (December 2025 preprint). [Record](https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.19076). A concrete neighboring classical lead emphasizing useful rather than merely shortest vectors. Reproduce and inspect the construction before generalizing it.
r1_priorart.md:528:**[Cleve]** Richard Cleve. *The query complexity of order-finding*. arXiv:quant-ph/9911124 (1999). [Record](https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9911124). Read the oracle model carefully. Its lower-bound conclusions do not automatically apply to algorithms exploiting the explicit representation of modular integers.
r1_priorart.md:532:## 9. Small exact smoke test
r1_priorart.md:592:## 10. Start here
Research task (use web search; read full papers, not just abstracts):
(1) Yann Tal, "Computing Modular Factorials Below the Square-Root Barrier", ECCC TR26-211 (Sept 2026), https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2026/211/ . (2) Bostan–Gaudry–Schost 2007 SIAM J. Comput., https://mathexp.eu/bostan/publications/BoGaSc07.pdf . (3) Harvey arXiv 2010.05450 and Harvey–Hittmeir arXiv 2105.11105.

Context: we want to decide, for an RSA-like N=pq with UNKNOWN factors, the predicate U(K) := [gcd((K-1)!, N) = 1] (equivalently: K <= least prime factor), ideally faster than computing (K-1)! mod N. Binary search on U gives the least prime factor. We do NOT need the factorial value, only unit/nonunit.

For Tal's paper: give a precise technical summary of the algorithm: every identity used (Jacobi sums, Gauss sums, reconstruction steps, recursive reductions), each step's cost, and for EACH step state exactly what it requires to know: the prime p itself, the factorization of p-1 or of some auxiliary integer, a primitive root / generator, multiplicative characters of known order, a field structure (division), etc. Then: which steps survive if computing modulo a composite N with unknown factorization (work in Z/NZ, treat failed inversions as factor discoveries)? Where exactly does the argument break? Is there any sub-step that computes a product of the form prod_{j in S} (x - j) or a factorial-like quantity modulo N with cost below sqrt(K)? Be concrete: name lemma/section numbers.

For BGS and Harvey/HH: extract exactly the operation that is batched (e.g., multipoint evaluation of prod (x+i) over a progression; baby-step/giant-step on polynomial matrices; Harvey's use of the structure of N ≡ ... and lattice/"Lehman-like" steps), the cost in terms of K or N, and what part of the method depends only on N (unknown factors) vs on hypotheses.

Then do original thinking: propose up to 3 concrete ideas for answering ONLY the unit predicate U(K) (or a weaker statement, e.g., "the interval [A,B) contains a divisor of N") more cheaply than the factorial value, e.g., using that a unit test only needs the product modulo an unknown prime to be nonzero, randomization, character sums whose evaluation is explicit from N alone, or polynomial identities in Z/NZ[x]. For each idea give the exact operation, cost in terms of K and log N, and where it fails. Label claims proved / conjectured / refuted. Output Markdown.

